  "fields": {
    "title": "Graphical User Interface",
    "textContent": {
      "nodeType": "document",
      "data": {},
      "content": [
        {
          "nodeType": "heading-3",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "Description",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "Graphical user interface (GUI) refers to a form of user interface on a computer. It has the function of making application software on a computer accessible by using graphic symbols, which are also called widgets. With computers, this is usually done by using a mouse as a control device with which the graphic elements are operated or selected; smartphones, tablets and other devices are usually operated by touching a sensor screen. The concept of the GUI became popular in 1984 with Apple's Macintosh, and developed into the industry standard for personal computers in the 1990s.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "heading-3",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "Background",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "The GUI was decisively shaped by the engineers Douglas Engelbart and Alan Kay, who saw the potential of computers to be a visual experience similar to television. Inspired by the realism of these media, they developed not only the GUI in the 1960s, but also the computer mouse, which was from then on used for interaction and navigation within the interface. This turned the computer into a visual medium that enabled ",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "2SDgfBJb0TqIw0u9MPTzLf",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "WYSIWYG",
                  "marks": [],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": " applications and was no longer just a ",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "5xxqnW1VsVWs2TSUh1pSzE",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "command-line operator",
                  "marks": [],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": ". Abstract data was transformed into graphics that imitated realistic objects, such as folders or paper baskets. This is how the so-called desktop metaphor was born.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "3hQwnJ8F2S1CDyfqJsOTRn",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "[1]",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "superscript"
                    }
                  ],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": " The style in which these graphics were often made is called ",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "2Ib0yeXE5iLTu4gX6TDg0e",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "Skeuomorphism",
                  "marks": [],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": ".",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "In the process of developing the GUI, Engelbart and Kay decided to call the opening rectangles on the computer windows rather than frames or something similar. Although this is only a metaphor, it has had a significant impact on how content is presented and perceived within a window. For this metaphor suggests that a window allows us to see data, text and images undistorted in their natural form, forgetting the interface through which we are looking. The operations and commands behind the GUI became irrelevant and difficult to understand. The GUI was meant to be as natural and intuitive as possible, making it easy for new users who were not familiar with the technology of a computer to get started. Because of this environment, it is not surprising that the first developers of the web designed websites in this spirit and zeitgeist - as a continuation of the GUI. The striving for ",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "6UzOyP2ueyoyoD8IMtYciu",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "transparency",
                  "marks": [],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": " thus became an important design philosophy within the discipline of webdesign and had a crucial influence on it.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "3hQwnJ8F2S1CDyfqJsOTRn",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
